Rational Design of a Metal-Organic Framework Host for Sulfur Storage in Fast, Long-Cycle Li-S Batteries
\(^{a}\) Received (in XXX, XXX) Xth XXXXXXXXX 20XX, Accepted Xth XXXXXXXXX 20XX DOI: 10.1039/b000000x
Unlike an intercalation cathode, which has an intrinsic host structure made of redox metal sites allowing the transport of \(\mathrm{Li^{+}/e^{-}}\), sulfur as a conversion cathode requires an additional host to store and immobilize the mobile redox centers, polysulfides. Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) as a class of highly porous and well-defined crystalline materials are a promising platform to search for an effective host through rational design. With properly selected electrolyte and cutoff voltage range, sulfur stored in an appropriate MOF host can take advantage of both intercalation (fast and stable) and conversion (high energy density) cathodes. Herein, we describe a fast cathode with long cycle life based on sulfur and ZIF-8 nanocrystals. With \(30\;\mathrm{wt\%}\) sulfur loading in electrode, it achieves remarkable discharge capacities of \(1,055\;\mathrm{mAh\;g^{-1}}\) (based on sulfur) at 0.1C and \(710\;\mathrm{mAh\;g^{-1}}\) at 1C. The decay over 300 cycles at 0.5C is \(0.08\%\) per cycle, prominent in long-cycle Li-S batteries. By comparing with another three distinct MOFs, MIL-53 (Al), \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-MIL-53 (Al) and HKUST-1, as well as two sets of ZIF-8 with particle sizes in the micrometer range, it reveals that (i) small particle size of the MOF host is appreciable to achieve a high capacity and (ii) small apertures, associated with functionalities in the open framework that have affinity with the polysulfide anions, can help achieve a stable cycling. We believe that the findings are general and applicable for the rational design of new hosts for sulfur in other porous materials families to produce more effective and stable Li-S batteries.
## Introduction
Having received great success in the past two decades in powering portable wireless devices such as mobile phones, laptops and digital cameras,\(^{1,2}\) cathode materials of the first generation rechargeable lithium-ion batteries (LIBs) are well-established on intercalation chemistry. Such a cathode (e.g. \(\mathrm{LiCoO}_{2}\)) consists of a host framework (layered host \(\mathrm{CoO}_{2}\) for \(\mathrm{LiCoO}_{2}\)) into/from which the mobile cation \((\mathrm{Li}^{+})\) is inserted/extracted reversibly.\(^{3}\) Electron transfer occurs with the redox-active transition metal \((\mathrm{Co(IV)/Co(III)}\) for \(\mathrm{LiCoO}_{2}\)) of the host, which is inevitably limited in capacity to one or at most two Li per host (only 0.5 for \(\mathrm{LiCoO}_{2}\)).\(^{4}\) For the growing demand in stored energy density for powering an electric vehicle (EV), attention has turned to the use of inexpensive multielectron redox centers based on conversion chemistry, where discharge/charge of the electrode is coupled with cleavage/formation of covalent bonds.\(^{3,5}\) Sulfur \((\mathrm{S}_{8})\) is one of the most promising candidates. With two Li per S atom, it provides a high theoretical capacity of \(1,675\;\mathrm{mAh\;g^{-1}}\) and specific energy of \(2,500\;\mathrm{Wh\;kg^{-1}}\), at least 3~5 times that of intercalation cathodes.\(^{5,6}\) Besides, it is abundant (with extremely low cost) and environmentally benign, both
important for a new energy economy.\(^{6}\) However, due to the intrinsic nature of the overall redox couple, described by the reaction \(\mathrm{S}_{8}+16\mathrm{Li}^{+}+16\mathrm{e}^{-}\leftrightarrow8\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}\), tremendous difficulties are involved in the design of such a conversion cathode with high specific capacity and long cycle life.\(^{7}\) A first one is related to the utilization of active materials. Because sulfur and its reduction products \((\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S})\) are both electronically and ionically insulating, it is strictly required that they are always in good contact with the conductive matrix. This is of particular difficulty to realize because polysulfides, the intermediate reaction species, are highly soluble in the electrolyte, which makes it easy for them to either react with the Li anode creating an internal shuttle phenomenon, or randomly precipitate on the cathode surface as insoluble agglomerates causing active mass loss. As a result, sulfur cathodes are often plagued with rapid capacity fading.
Unlike an intercalation cathode, which has an inherent host structure made of redox metal sites allowing the transport of \(\mathrm{Li^{+}/e^{-}}\) with minor perturbation to the framework, sulfur as a conversion cathode requires an additional host to store and immobilize the mobile redox centers, namely, polysulfides.\(^{8}\) Carbonaceous materials, such as porous carbon,\(^{9,10}\) carbon nanofibers,\(^{11,12}\) carbon nanotubes,\(^{13,14}\) carbon spheres,\(^{15,16}\) and various types of graphene,\(^{17-19}\) have been extensively investigated
as effective hosts in sulfur cathodes, for their high electronic conductivity and physical adsorption/absorption on polysulfides. Similar role can be played by conducting polymers to get enhanced cycling stability.\(^{20, 21}\) Surface coating, with either polymer\(^{22}\) or metal oxide,\(^{23, 24}\) is another strategy to provide a host. The coating layer serves as a physical barrier to prevent the polysulfides from leaving the cathode, yet in some cases at the expense of retarding the transport of \(\mathrm{Li^{+}/e^{-}}\).\(^{23}\) The use of an adsorbing additive to reversibly capture and release polysulfides during operation is a design of an auxiliary host.\(^{25, 26}\) One notable example for this strategy is SBA-15,\(^{25}\) termed as a "polysulfide reservoir" by the authors, which stabilizes the polysulfide anions through weak binding on the positively charged silica surface. A generalized host, with modifications of the cell configuration, is given by inserting a carbon paper between the cathode and the separator, which traps the polysulfides within the cathode region.\(^{27, 28}\) To sum, a host material for sulfur storage must have "smart" pore structure and "suitable" chemical environment to give "appropriate" interaction with the polysulfide anions. Though the abovementioned materials have satisfied these requirements to a large extent, rational and systematic design and adjustment of the size, shape, functionality of the pores and thus interaction with the polysulfides in these materials is hard to carry out due to their synthetic methodologies.\(^{25}\) Encouragingly, coworkers with Tarascon and Férey pioneered the use of metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) as host materials for sulfur storage, taking advantage of the weak binding between the polysulfides and the oxygenated framework.\(^{29}\) By using a mesoporous MOF, MIL-100 (Cr), the authors showed a remarkable increase in capacity retention of the cell. After that, various groups have demonstrated that other MOFs can be adopted.\(^{30-32}\) Since MOFs are a class of porous materials assembled by connecting metal ions and organic linkers with tremendous extensiveness in variety and multiplicity,\(^{33-35}\) there is great opportunity to rationally design and systematically adjust for an effective MOF host to store sulfur and fully confine the mobile redox centers, supported by the state-of-the-art science and technology in the reticular chemistry.\(^{35}\) Logical examinations of structure-property trends to suggest promising candidates are therefore desirable. Yet to design an efficient MOF host for sulfur storage, factors beyond MOF structures also need considerations, as depicted in Figure 1. Above all is the particle size of the host. Lessons from the intercalation cathode design tell us that the rate of \(\mathrm{Li^{+}/e^{-}}\) transport can be significantly increased by reducing the dimensions of the material, because the characteristic time constant \(t\) for diffusion is proportional to the square of the diffusion length \(L\) (\(t \approx L^{2}/D\)), where \(D\) is the ion diffusivity;\(^{2, 4}\) thus small dimensions for the host are desired. Secondly, the \(\mathrm{Li^{+}/e^{-}}\) transport is coupled with the reactions in the redox cascade; their kinetics: all-liquid \((\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{8} \leftrightarrow \mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{6} \leftrightarrow \mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{4})\) > liquid-solid \((\mathrm{S}_{8} \leftrightarrow \mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{8}, \mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{4} \leftrightarrow \mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{2})\) >> all-solid \((\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{2} \leftrightarrow \mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S})\).\(^{5, 8}\) The kinetics, while influenced by the pore structure and chemical environment of the host, is also determined by the selection of electrolyte\(^{10, 36}\) and cutoff voltage range.\(^{37}\) The existence of soluble polysulfides in the system is valuable for maintaining a fast kinetics, owing to the molecular nature of the species involved so that they can rapidly exchange \(\mathrm{Li^{+}/e^{-}}\) with each
other.\(^{5, 38}\) Comprehensive optimization of these factors can give birth to a fast and stable sulfur cathode, in which the mobile dielectron redox centers are well-confined in a well-defined host framework for the reversible insertion/extraction of \(\mathrm{Li^{+}/e^{-}}\). MOF here builds the bridge between intercalation and conversion cathodes.
\(^{5}\) Former MOF hosts\(^{29-31}\) fall short in (i) fast initial decay during early cycling (30~70%), (ii) low working current density (most at 0.1 C), and (iii) low sulfur loading (<20 wt% in electrode). Herein, we demonstrate a fast sulfur cathode with long cycle life by using ZIF-8 nanocrystals as the host. With 30 wt% sulfur loading in electrode, prominent discharge capacities of 1,055 mAh g\(^{-1}\) (based on sulfur) at 0.1 C and 710 mAh g\(^{-1}\) at 1 C are achieved in an electrolyte with low viscosity and high polysulfide solubility. Most impressively, the capacity decay over 300 cycles at 0.5 C is as small as 0.08% per cycle, among the excellent retention in long-cycle Li-S batteries. It is also worthy to note that by testing within 1.8~2.8 V, rapid initial decays are generally avoided.
To broaden our knowledge of how the structures of the MOF hosts are linked with their properties in the Li-S system, data are presented with another three distinct MOFs, MIL-53 (Al), \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-MIL-53 (Al), and HKUST-1. These MOFs represent a cross-section of unique characteristics such as breathing network with 1D channels (MIL-53\(^{39}\) and \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-MIL-53\(^{40}\)), amine functionality (\(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-MIL-53), unsaturated metal sites (HKUST-1\(^{41}\)) and cage-type pores with small apertures (ZIF-8\(^{42}\)), which are illustrated in Figure 2. In particular, they all have reasonable thermal\(^{39-42}\) and chemical stability toward sulfur and other chemicals involved in the synthetic procedures and electrochemical tests. It is also noteworthy that these MOFs are simple, scalable, and cost-effective: all are advantageous for practical use. To clarify the contribution of MOF particle size to the electrochemical performance, two sets of homogeneous microsized ZIF-8 particles with different average sizes are used to compare with their nanosized counterpart.
## Experimental Section
## \(\mathbf{\nabla}^{5}\) Synthesis
MIL-53 (Al), \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-MIL-53 (Al), HKUST-1 as well as two sets of microsized ZIF-8 (denoted as ZIF-8-M and ZIF-8-L) were synthesized through solution methods.\(^{43-45}\) Nanocrystals of ZIF-8 were yielded via a simple mechanochemical synthetic protocol.\(^{46}\) It is preparing a sulfur-MOF composite (S/MOF), sulfur was first ground with degassed MOF powders at predetermined weight ratio (1:1), followed by a conventional melt diffusion process\(^{9, 29}\) under modified temperatures (details see ESI†). All the degassed MOFs and S/MOF samples were kept in an argon-filled glove box prior use.
## Characterization
The structure and morphology of the samples were characterized by powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD, Rigaku D/max 2000 diffractometer, Cu Kα) and scanning electron microscope (SEM, Hitachi S4800). Nitrogen adsorption-desorption isotherms were measured on a COULTER SA 3100 apparatus at 77 K. Before the measurement, MOF samples were degassed with reported procedures;\(^{42-44}\) S/MOF samples were measured immediately after leaving the glove box. Thermal
gravimetric analysis (TGA) was carried out on a Q600 SDT thermoanalyzer (Thermal Analysis Corporation, USA) in \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) with a heating rate of \(10\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C/min}\) : all samples were tested immediately after leaving the glove box.
## 5 Electrochemistry
To prepare the cathodes, \(60\,\mathrm{\wt\%}\) S/MOF composite, \(30\,\mathrm{\wt\%}\) conductive carbon black (CB) and \(10\ \mathrm{\wt\%}\) poly(vinylidene fluoride) (PVDF) binder were mixed in N-methyl-2-pyrrolidinone (NMP) to form a slurry. The slurry was then coated on aluminium current collectors and dried at \(60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) over \(24~\mathrm{h}\) . Coin cells of 2032-type with a metallic Li anode were assembled. \(0.6~\mathrm{M}\) bis(trifluoromethanesulfonyl)imide lithium (LiTESI) in a mixed solvent of 1,2-dimethoxyethane (DME) and 1,3-dioxolane (DOL) \((\mathrm{v/v}\ 1{:}1)\) was used as the electrolyte with \(0.1\,\mathrm{~M~LiNO}_{3}\) as an additive  to  passivate  the  surface  of  the  Li  anode.47  This electrolyte was chosen for its low viscosity and high polysulfide solubility.48 The total amount of electrolyte added to each cell was \(60\,\upmu\mathrm{L}\) . The batteries were cycled galvanostatically with a LAND CT2001A instrument (Wuhan, China). Electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) measurements were performed on an electrochemical workstation (CH I660D, Shanghai Chenhua) within the frequency range of \(100\ \mathrm{kHz}\) to \(0.1\ \mathrm{Hz}\) with an AC voltage amplitude of \(5\;\mathrm{mV}\) . The batteries were first discharged to \(1.8\;\mathrm{V}\) then charged to \(2.8~\mathrm{V}\) at 0.1C for activation. After that, EIS measurements were performed at \(0\%\) , \({\sim}20\%\) , \({\sim}80\%\) and \(100\%\) depth of discharge and then \({\sim}50\%\) , \({\sim}80\%\) and \(100\%\) depth of charge, subsequently, during the second cycle at 0.1C. All the tests were carried out at room temperature.
## Results and discussion
30 By virtue of the mechanochemical synthesis, ZIF-8 with uniform particle size \((100{\sim}200~\mathrm{~nm})\) and morphology were obtained  (Figure  3a),  valuable  for  improving  electrode homogeneity and integrity.49, 50 Scanning electron microscopy (SEM) studies suggested that the size and morphology of S/ZIF-8 35 (Figure 3b) remain almost the same as ZIF-8 alone (Figure 3a); no  large  sulfur  agglomerates  were  found.  However,  closer observation reveals that some sulfur still exists in a highly dispersed state on the external surface, which is commonly unavoidable  in  one-step  heating  treatment.16,  17  Elemental 40 mapping of sulfur in SEM also confirmed its uniform distribution (Figure 3c). The impregnation of sulfur was next studied by powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD). As shown in Figure 3d, the structure of ZIF-8 is well-maintained during the whole process; on the other hand, characteristic peaks for bulk crystalline sulfur 55 become almost undetectable after the heating step, indicating the success of sulfur infiltration.29 The sulfur infiltration was further investigated by nitrogen adsorption measurements9 (Figure 3e): S/ZIF-8 shows significant loss in porosity compared with pristine ZIF-8. The sulfur content, both calculated by the mass changes 50 before and after the heating treatment15 and measured by thermal gravimetric analysis (TGA, Figure 3f), agrees well with the predetermined one \((\sim\!50\,\mathrm{\wt\%}\) of the composite). Similar results were obtained for the sulfur loading of MIL-53 (Figure S1), \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) - MIL-53 (Figure S2), HKUST-1 (Figure S3), ZIF-8-M (Figure S4) 55 and ZIF-8-L (Figure S5).
S/MOFs were first cycled between 1.8 and \(2.8\,\mathrm{~V~}\) at various
discharge/charge rates from 0.1C to 1C then back to 0.1C (Figure 4a). Contrary to the previously reported S/MIL-100,29 where the highest capacity was achieved in the first discharge followed by 60 an abrupt drop, all the S/MOFs in our study exhibit an activation process in the initial period. This phenomenon was previously found in systems where the accessibility of sulfur to the electrolyte is limited at the beginning;50 owing to the smaller pore sizes of our MOFs comparing to MIL-100, along with the higher sulfur loadings, it takes time for sulfur inside the pores to become fully electrochemically active. The maximum discharge capacities achieved after the activation process are 1,215, 1,125 and \(1,055\,\mathrm{\mAh\g^{-1}}\) at 0.1C for S/MIL-53, \(\mathrm{S/NH}_{2}\) -MIL-53 and S/ZIF-8, respectively. Such high utilizations of sulfur in these 70 S/MOFs demonstrate that even sulfur stored in an electronically insulating microporous host can still be highly electrochemically active in an electrolyte with low viscosity and high polysulfide solubility.48 However, the maximum discharge capacity for S/HKUST-1 is only as low as \(526~\mathrm{mAh~g^{-1}}\) at 0.1C. This can be 75 ascribed to the extraordinarily large particle size of over \(10~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}\) for HKUST-1 crystals (Figure S3a): according to \(t\approx L^{2}/D,^{2,4}L\) for HKUST-1 is at least two times that of MIL-53, the second largest MOF in our study (in particle size, Figure S1a); thus \(t\) for HKUST-1 is at least four times that of MIL-53, extremely 80 unfavorable for \(\mathrm{Li^{+}/e^{-}}\) transport. Impressively, the small ZIF-8 nanocrystals of \(100{\sim}200\,\mathrm{~nm}\) yielded via the mechanochemical synthesis show excellent rate capabilities: even at a high discharge/charge rate of 1C, S/ZIF-8 can still deliver a high reversible capacity of more than \(710\ \mathrm{mAh\g^{-1}}\) . More importantly, 85 over \(950\ \mathrm{mAh\g^{-1}}\) can be recovered when the current rate was switched back to 0.1 C again, demonstrating the robustness of the MOF host in response to abrupt current changes. After 60 cycles at 0.1C, the capacity still stands at \(710\ \mathrm{mAh\g^{-1}}\) , exceeding MIL\(100^{29}\) in both electrode capacity and cycling stability.
