facilitating \(\mathrm{Li^{+}/e^{-}}\) transport across shorter dimensions. S/MIL-53, though exhibiting high \(U_{1}\) of \(2.43\,\mathrm{~V~}\) and considerable \(Q_{2}/Q_{1}\) of 2.3 at 0.1C, suffers from a fast drop in \(Q_{2}/Q_{1}\) upon increase of the current rate; things are even worse in its counterpart, \(\mathrm{S/NH}_{2}\) -MIL- 5 53, accompanied with a rapid decline in \(U_{1}\) . Clearly, the 1D pathways in these two MOFs are rather disadvantageous for ion motion, like the case in bulk \(\mathrm{LiFePO_{4}}\) .52 In addition, the Lewis basic amino groups in \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -MIL-53, while in one hand are unfavorable for the generation and stabilization of the polysulfide 10 phase, raising the energy barrier for charge transfer,38 in another hand increase the hindrance of the MOF channels, dampening the ion diffusion. In contrast, the Lewis acidic open metal sites in HKUST-1,44 while on interfacial kinetics, are beneficial, for stabilizing the negatively charged redox centers, on internal \(\mathrm{Li^{+}/e^{-}}\) 15 transport, however, are unfavorable, for anchoring the anions and retarding their exchange of \(\mathrm{Li^{+}/e^{-}}\) . The former effect is suggested by the smallest decrease in \(U_{1}\) among the four S/MOFs, while the latter is evident by the lowest \(Q_{2}/Q_{1}\) of 2.0 even at 0.1C.
To study how the sizes of MOF hosts affect the performance, 20 microsized ZIF-8, ZIF-8-M (Figure S4) and ZIF-8-L (Figure S5), with an average size of \(1\;{\upmu\mathrm{m}}\) and \(3\;{\upmu\mathrm{m}}\) , respectively, are used as a prototype in comparison with their nanosized counterpart (150 nm in average). The three S/ZIF-8 composites were tested under a constant discharge/charge rate of 0.5C for 100 cycles (Figure 25 5a). In spite of the similar cycling stabilities (indeed downsized MOFs show a bit better retention), the major difference made by particle size lies in reversible capacities: while \(150\;\mathrm{nm}\) sized ZIF8 can reach a discharge capacity of \(733\mathrm{\mAh\g^{-1}}\) , ZIF-8 of \(1\ \upmu\mathrm{m}\) only delivers \(556\;\mathrm{mAh\;g^{-1}}\) , this value further drops to \(491\;\mathrm{mAh\;g^{-}}\) 1 when particle sizes increase to \(3\ \upmu\mathrm{m}\) (Figure 5c, black bars). Particle size does not give rise to significant change in the shapes of voltage profiles (Figure 5b); yet two trends are noteworthy. First, voltage hysteresis loops enlarge with downsized MOFs, indicating more and more insufficient electron transport from the 35 given amount of conductive agent. This issue is common in nanoparticles since they have fewer points of contact with the conductive network to ensure electron transport than microsized materials.4 Second, the ratio of \(Q_{2}/Q_{1}\) increases with the downsizing of MOFs (Figure 5c red bars). This feature, along 40 with the higher reversible capacity, is the core benefit given by downsized MOFs: shorter diffusion lengths strongly facilitate the utilization of sulfur – more sulfur becomes electrochemically active with more \(\mathrm{Li^{+}/e^{-}}\) per S atom. Overall, changing the particle sizes of MOF hosts mainly affect the internal \(\mathrm{Li^{+}/e^{-}}\) transport, 45 thus in turn the reversible capacity; cycling stability on the other hand receives less impact from MOF particle size compared with MOF structure itself – at least in the range of \(100\ \mathrm{nm}\) to \(3\ \upmu\mathrm{m}\) . Therefore, if one is to preliminarily search for a potential candidate of MOF host, particle size of the MOF can be taken 50 into consideration as a secondary factor; only when one is to fully optimize the performance of a certain MOF should the particle size be carefully tailored.
The  S/MOFs  were  next  tested  under  a  constant discharge/charge rate of 0.5C over 300 cycles to examine their 55 long-term cyclabilities (Figure 6a). The maximum discharge capacities achieved during the cycling process are 738, 431, 568 and \(793\,\mathrm{\mAh\g^{-1}}\) for S/ZIF-8, S/HKUST-1, \(\mathrm{S/NH}_{2}\) -MIL-53 and S/MIL-53, respectively. After the initial stages for activation, all
S/MOFs show remarkable capacity retention over prolonged 0 cycling. At the end of 300 cycles, their discharge capacities are 553, 286, 332 and \(347\;\mathrm{mAh\;g^{-1}}\) for S/ZIF-8, S/HKUST-1, \(\mathrm{S/NH}_{2}\) - MIL-53 and S/MIL-53, respectively, accounting for \(75\%\) , \(66\%\) , \(58\%\) and \(44\%\) of their maximum capacities.
The fading in the four S/MOF systems seems to be directly linked with the window sizes of the MOF pores (Figure 6b). The diameters of the largest aperture (Figure 6c) in ZIF-8, HKUST-1, \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -MIL-53 and MIL-53 are \(3.4^{,42}\ 6.9^{,43}\ 7.5^{,53}\) and \(8.5\,\mathrm{~\AA~}\) ,39 respectively, corresponding to the average decay rates of \(0.08\%\) , \(0.11\%\) , \(0.14\%\) , and \(0.19\%\) per cycle at 0.5C and \(0.43\%\) , \(0.45\%\) , \(0.72\%\) and \(0.82\%\) per cycle at 0.1C. MIL-100, which has a hexagonal window of \(8.6\,\mathrm{~\AA~}^{29}\) has a similar decay rate \((\sim0.83\%\) per cycle at 0.1C, initial decay was excluded) with MIL-53, but decays faster than the other three MOFs. Specifically, the diameter of a \(S_{8}\) ring is \(6.9\,\mathrm{~\AA~}\) ;13 this may suggest as a first approximation that the immobilizing effect given by an open framework with window size larger than \(6.9\,\mathrm{~\AA~}\) would be substantially weakened (as displayed by MIL-53 and \(\mathrm{MIL}{-}100^{29}\) ). Note that the decay rates at 0.1C are approximately five times those at 0.5C for S/ZIF-8 and \(\mathrm{S/NH}_{2}\) -MIL-53, which may imply 0 that the leaching of polysulfides from the two MOF hosts is just proportional to the time for them to leach, and that the chemical environment inside the two MOFs has no additional gain in polysulfide confinement. Comparatively, this factor is 3.9 and 4.4 for S/HKUST-1 and S/MIL-53, respectively, indicating some 5 extra immobilizing effect from the internal environment. During the review process, we noticed that a very recent work showed an excellent MOF host with a large window size of \(13.8\mathrm{~\AA~}\) giving high cycling stability.32 This can be taken as an example of strong immobilizing effect posed by the internal environment (strong 0 Lewis acidic centers) to compensate its relatively large apertures.
Figure 7 illustrates the schematic model of the S/MOF system in the discharge process. Because the MOF host is electronically insulating, electrochemical reactions initiate on the surfaces of MOFs, more specifically, the entrances of the pores, where the 5 active mass can get access to \(\mathrm{Li^{+}/e^{-}}\) . Sulfur in the outermost areas of the MOF particles is first reduced to long-chain polysulfides. Once generated, they can: (i) exchange \(\mathrm{Li^{+}/e^{-}}\) with sulfur (or polysulfides) through the MOF channels (internal transport), (ii) be directly reduced to shorter chain species by the electrode 10 (interfacial charge transfer), and (iii) escape from the MOF host (escape diffusion). While the escape diffusion (iii) is directly linked with capacity fading, the competition between internal transport (i) and interfacial charge transfer (ii) determines the amount of capacity that can be delivered before the total reaction 15 is terminated by the complete blockage of the pores with the generation of \(\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{2}/\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}\) .54, 55
To help evaluate the relative importance of these processes, electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) measurements were carried out at different discharge and charge states during 10 the 2nd cycle at 0.1C after the initial activation (Figure 8). Only one semicircle in the high-frequency (HF) region reflecting the charge transfer process at the conductive matrix interface is observed among all S/MOFs at all states; the semicircle normally in the middle-frequency (MF) range attributed to the formation of 15 an insulating film on the carbon matrix does not appear.50, 54 According to the fitting analysis of the Nyquist plots, the solution
resistances \((R_{\mathrm{s}})\) for all S/MOFs amount to a negligible contribution to the total resistance and do not show significant change during the whole process, indicating that the MOF hosts are effective in suppressing the escape diffusion (iii). The charge transfer resistances \((R_{\mathrm{ct}})\), in contrast, vary a lot among different S/MOFs, from over a thousand Ohms in S/ZIF-8 to several tens of Ohms in S/HKUST-1. The combination of the fastest interfacial kinetics and the lowest specific capacity for S/HKUST-1 here again emphasizes the importance of small dimensions to reduce \(\mathrm{Li^{+}/e^{-}}\) transport distances. Also the best performance of S/ZIF-8 associated with the highest \(R_{\mathrm{ct}}\) indicates that the internal transport (i) is more likely to be the limiting factor in fully utilizing the active materials than the interfacial charge transfer (ii). Note that in all S/MOFs, \(R_{\mathrm{ct}}\) reaches the minimum at the end of discharge, indicating that considerable amount of soluble species still exist at the cutoff voltage of \(1.8\;\mathrm{V}\) (Remember that polysulfides facilitate charge transfer!) as we wanted and the blockage of the pores by the insoluble species is not severe. This can explain the systematic improvement in cycling reversibility among all S/MOFs just by setting the cutoff voltage for discharge at \(1.8\;\mathrm{V}\).
We have also tested the batteries between 1.6 and \(2.8\,\mathrm{~V~}\) at various discharge/charge rates. Figure 9 shows the results at 0.1C. At first glance, the cycling behaviors are quite similar with those previously reported ones,29-31 that is, a sharp decay occurs in the early period before the cycling becomes stable: \(27\%{\sim}57\%\) in Figure 8e, \({\sim}30\%\) for MIE, \(100,^{29}\,{>}\,70\%\) for HKUST- \(1^{30}\) and \({>}50\%\) for ZIF-8.31 Sulfur loading may become the first concern (the sulfur loading effect is discussed in detail in ESI, see Figure S6). Since the sulfur loadings in our study, both in the composite (50 \(\mathrm{wt}\%\) ) and in the cathode \((30\,\mathrm{\wt\%})\) , are higher than previous reports,29-31 if the sulfur on the external surfaces of the MOF particles  is  the  major  contributor  to  the  initial  decay,  a systematically larger decay would be observed in our S/MOFs, but this is not the case. A second concern may come from the concentration  of  LiTFSI  salt.  We  used  a  lower  molarity electrolyte \((0.6\;\mathrm{M})\) compared with those reports \((1\;\mathrm{M})\) .29-31 Since a low molarity electrolyte tends to dissolve more polysulfides and leads to more severe capacity fadings,10, 36 clearly, it cannot explain the absence of intense fading in our study when cutoff voltage for discharge was set at \(1.8\;\mathrm{V}\) . Careful comparison of the voltage profiles in Figure 4 and 9 reveals that a significant amount of capacity is released within the voltage range between \(1.6\;\mathrm{V}\) and \(1.8\;\mathrm{V}\) in the first discharge, which substantially shrinks in  the  second  cycle.  This  irreversible  capacity  cannot  be attributed to the reduction of \(\mathrm{LiNO}_{3}\) , because (i) irreversible capacities given by \(\mathrm{LiNO}_{3}\) are often present as two long voltage plateaus at \(1.6~\mathrm{V}\) and \(1.3\mathrm{~V~}\) ,56 and (ii) the same concentration of \(\mathrm{LiNO}_{3}\) was also used as an additive in previous reports and no irreversible  capacities  were  present  even  the  batteries  were discharged to \(1.0\;\mathrm{V}\) .30, 31
We interpret this irreversible capacity as follows, and a schematic model is illustrated in Figure 9f. Because of the high sulfur contents in our S/MOFs (little room for accommodation of the volume change), the entrances of the MOF pores are easier to be blocked by the formation of insoluble species in the late period of discharge, since the volume expansion caused by lithiation initiates from the outermost areas of the particles. When the
surfaces of a MOF particle are fully covered by the insoluble 60 species, its core areas still remain highly lithium deficient. Deeper discharge needs to overcome the barriers of this surface coating, for the transport of both electrons and Li ions, rendering an extra polarization. After discharged to \(1.6~\mathrm{V}\) , the whole MOF pores are full of insoluble \(\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{2}/\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}\) mixture; because of its 65 lower  density,  much  of  it  spreads  out  of  the  pores  and accumulates on the surfaces, leading to a volume expansion of the whole particle. This expansion causes (i) a great damage to the electrical contact with the conductive matrix, and (ii) much of the  active  mass  to  escape  from  the  MOF  host;  both  are 70 responsible for the large initial decay.24, 50 Therefore a deep discharge definitely weakens the advantage of a MOF host.
Another disadvantage associated with the deep discharge is that a large potential barrier is apparent up at the beginning of the first charge (Figure 9a-d), which does not occur if discharged only to \(1.8\,\mathrm{~V~}\) (Figure 4b-e). Lack of polysulfide phase, the oxidation of \(\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{2}/\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}\) is extremely sluggish, which has been fully discussed in previous literature.38 This emphasizes the important role of dissolved polysulfides in exchanging \(\mathrm{Li^{+}/e^{-}}\) and accelerating the electrochemical kinetics. Here to set the cutoff 80 voltage for discharge to \(1.8\ \mathrm{~V~}\) provides a mild activation procedure in a way that polysulfides are always available in the MOF channels to facilitate the kinetics both in charge and discharge.
The addition of \(\mathrm{LiNO}_{3}\) can be a considerable factor in the S/MOF system. According to previous reports,12, 32, 57 \(\mathrm{LiNO}_{3}\) as an electrolyte additive mainly helps to enhance the Coulombic efficiency, whereas no improvement was shown on cycling stability solely based on the addition of \(\mathrm{LiNO}_{3}\) .58 Since the introduction of MOFs brings further complexity to the Li-S 90 system, to fully understand the effect of each component and the underlying mechanism in such system remains challenging. We did not rule out the synergetic effect of \(\mathrm{LiNO}_{3}\) when MOF was used as the host for sulfur. Hence, we carefully evaluated the dependence of electrochemical performance of S/ZIF-8 on the 95 addition of \(\mathrm{LiNO}_{3}\) (Figure S7). The shapes of voltage profiles are similar (Figure S7a and b), indicating no significant influence by \(\mathrm{LiNO}_{3}\) on the electrochemical reactions of active sulfur in the presence of MOF. Cycling stability does not show significant change in the absence of \(\mathrm{LiNO}_{3}\) (Figure S7c); Coulombic 100 efficiency, however, is the main difference, as the average level decreases from c.a. \(101\%\) (with \(\mathrm{LiNO}_{3}\) ) to c.a. \(95\%\) (without \(\mathrm{LiNO}_{3}\) ). These results are in line with previous reports;12, 32, 57 therefore it is reasonable to conclude that the main contributor to the stable cycling behaviour in the S/MOF system is the 105 confinement effect of MOF. However, there is no doubt that further research and systematic study are needed to fully understand the fundamental mechanism.
## Conclusions
MOFs are a promising platform to search for an effective host 110 to store sulfur and retain the soluble polysulfides through rational design and systematic adjustment. In conjunction with an electrolyte highly dissolving polysulfides and a proper voltage range of \(1.8{\sim}2.8\;\mathrm{~V~}\) , sulfur stored in a MOF host can take advantage of both intercalation (fast and stable) and conversion 115 (high energy density) cathodes, for the mobile dielectron redox
centers are well-trapped in the well-defined host for the reversible and efficient insertion/extraction of \(\mathrm{Li^{+}/e^{-}}\). We have demonstrated ZIF-8 nanocrystals, made from a simple mechanochemical synthetic protocol, an effective host. Prominent rate capabilities with exceptional capacity retention are achieved by virtue of a small window size of \(3.4\mathrm{\AA}\) plus a small and uniform particle size of \(100{\sim}200\ \mathrm{nm}\). By comparing with another three distinct MOFs, MIL-53 (Al), \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-MIL-53 (Al), and HKUST-1, along with two microsized ZIF-8 with different average particle sizes, useful guides for the rational design of a MOF host are elucidated. The performance of the as-designed cathode is governed by the internal transport of \(\mathrm{Li^{+}/e^{-}}\) and the escape diffusion of polysulfides; the former determines the utilization of sulfur, and the latter dominates capacity retention. Decreasing the particle size of the MOF host is appreciable to achieve a high capacity. Small apertures, associated with functionalities in the open framework that have affinity with the polysulfide anions, can help achieve a stable cycling. Further directions for developing the S/MOF system are straightforward: (i) applying more effective and flexible conductive matrix, such as carbon nanotubes and graphene, to enhance the overall conductivity, (ii) preparing smaller nanoparticles of MOFs to fully optimize sulfur utilization and rate capabilities, and (iii) using MOFs with giant cages (and small windows) to increase sulfur loading. Specifically, in the case of HKUST-1, as expected, the open metal centers serve as the Lewis acidic sites with high affinity to the negatively charged polysulfide anions and largely enhance capacity retention. In order to fully understand the effect of the metal centers, it is necessary to compare them in series of MOFs with identical underlying topology. By doing so, one can rule out the impact of pores and organic links. We believe that these guides are general and applicable for the rational design of new hosts for sulfur in other porous materials families to produce more effective and stable Li-S batteries.
